WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.150
Network telemetry provides insight into communications between systems, services, users,

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:06.685
and external destinations.

00:00:06.685 --> 00:00:11.280
Sources may include firewall events, DNS activity, proxy logs, network flow records,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.480
secure access systems, load balancers, and selected packet or protocol metadata.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:19.055
This information can reveal communication patterns, denied connections,

00:00:19.055 --> 00:00:23.770
unusual destinations, changes in volume, or unexpected relationships between assets.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:27.675
Network data often helps analysts connect activity across systems even when detailed

00:00:27.675 --> 00:00:29.360
endpoint events are unavailable,

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.835
though encrypted traffic and changing architectures can limit what is visible.

00:00:32.835 --> 00:00:36.300
Cloud telemetry includes control-plane audit logs, identity events,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.365
workload and service logs, configuration changes, storage access, API calls,

00:00:40.365 --> 00:00:41.690
and security findings.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:45.390
Because cloud services are dynamic, collection should account for multiple accounts,

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.975
subscriptions, regions, tenants, and service-specific logging options.

00:00:48.975 --> 00:00:53.075
Modern security programs combine on-premises and cloud telemetry to support hybrid

00:00:53.075 --> 00:00:54.135
investigations.

00:00:54.135 --> 00:00:57.860
Useful results depend on consistent identifiers, synchronized time,

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:01.820
asset and identity context, and an understanding of which provider, customer,

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.790
or service is responsible for each part of collection and retention.

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.949
Shared responsibility is especially important in cloud environments.

00:01:07.949 --> 00:01:11.825
A provider may generate an audit event, while the customer must enable export,

00:01:11.825 --> 00:01:14.870
choose a destination, set retention, and control access.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:18.980
Service onboarding should verify these responsibilities in practice so that a documented

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.515
logging requirement actually results in searchable,

00:01:21.515 --> 00:01:23.100
protected, and timely evidence.
